      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To find the length of the parametric curve described by \\((x, y) = (2 \\sin t, 2 \\cos t)\\) from \\(t = 0\\) to \\(t = \\pi\\), we need to use the formula for the arc length of a parametric curve. The arc length \\(L\\) of a curve given by parametric equations \\(x = x(t)\\) and \\(y = y(t)\\) from \\(t = a\\) to \\(t = b\\) is given by:\n\\[ L = \\int_{a}^{b} \\sqrt{\\left( \\frac{dx}{dt} \\right)^2 + \\left( \\frac{dy}{dt} \\right)^2} \\, dt \\]\n\nFor the parametric equations \\(x = 2 \\sin t\\) and \\(y = 2 \\cos t\\), we first need to find the derivatives of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) with respect to \\(t\\):\n\\[ \\frac{dx}{dt} = 2 \\cos t \\]\n\\[ \\frac{dy}{dt} = -2 \\sin t \\]\n\nNow we substitute these derivatives into the arc length formula:\n\\[ L = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{(2 \\cos t)^2 + (-2 \\sin t)^2} \\, dt \\]\n\\[ L = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{4 \\cos^2 t + 4 \\sin^2 t} \\, dt \\]\n\\[ L = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{4 (\\cos^2 t + \\sin^2 t)} \\, dt \\]\n\\[ L = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{4 \\cdot 1} \\, dt \\]\n\\[ L = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} 2 \\, dt \\]\n\\[ L = 2 \\int_{0}^{\\pi} dt \\]\n\\[ L = 2 \\left[ t \\right]_{0}^{\\pi} \\]\n\\[ L = 2 (\\pi - 0) \\]\n\\[ L = 2 \\pi \\]\n\nTherefore, the length of the parametric curve from \\(t = 0\\) to \\(t = \\pi\\) is \\(\\boxed{2 \\pi}\\).",
