      "text": "To find the length of the parametric curve described by \\( (x, y) = (2 \\sin t, 2 \\cos t) \\) from \\( t = 0 \\) to \\( t = \\pi \\), we use the parameterization of the curve given by:\n\n\\[\nx = 2 \\sin t \\quad \\text{and} \\quad y = 2 \\cos t.\n\\]\n\nThe formula for the arc length \\( L \\) of a parametric curve from \\( t = a \\) to \\( t = b \\) is:\n\n\\[\nL = \\int_a^b \\sqrt{\\left( \\frac{dx}{dt} \\right)^2 + \\left( \\frac{dy}{dt} \\right)^2} \\, dt.\n\\]\n\nFirst, we need to compute the derivatives of \\( x \\) and \\( y \\) with respect to \\( t \\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{dx}{dt} = \\frac{d}{dt}(2 \\sin t) = 2 \\cos t,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{dy}{dt} = \\frac{d}{dt}(2 \\cos t) = -2 \\sin t.\n\\]\n\nNext, we square these derivatives and add them together:\n\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{dx}{dt} \\right)^2 = (2 \\cos t)^2 = 4 \\cos^2 t,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{dy}{dt} \\right)^2 = (-2 \\sin t)^2 = 4 \\sin^2 t.\n\\]\n\nAdding these results:\n\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{dx}{dt} \\right)^2 + \\left( \\frac{dy}{dt} \\right)^2 = 4 \\cos^2 t + 4 \\sin^2 t = 4 (\\cos^2 t + \\sin^2 t).\n\\]\n\nWe know from the Pythagorean identity that:\n\n\\[\n\\cos^2 t + \\sin^2 t = 1.\n\\]\n\nThus,\n\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{dx}{dt} \\right)^2 + \\left( \\frac{dy}{dt} \\right)^2 = 4 \\cdot 1 = 4.\n\\]\n\nNow, substitute this back into the arc length formula:\n\n\\[\nL = \\int_0^\\pi \\sqrt{4} \\, dt = \\int_0^\\pi 2 \\, dt.\n\\]\n\nEvaluating this integral:\n\n\\[\nL = 2 \\int_0^\\pi dt = 2 \\left[ t \\right]_0^\\pi = 2 (\\pi - 0) = 2\\pi.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the length of the parametric curve is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\pi}.\n\\]",
