      "text": "To find the length of the parametric curve \\((x, y) = (2 \\sin t, 2 \\cos t)\\) from \\(t = 0\\) to \\(t = \\pi\\), we use the formula for the arc length of a parametric curve:\n\n\\[ L = \\int_{a}^{b} \\sqrt{\\left( \\frac{dx}{dt} \\right)^2 + \\left( \\frac{dy}{dt} \\right)^2} \\, dt \\]\n\nGiven the parametric equations:\n\\[ x = 2 \\sin t \\]\n\\[ y = 2 \\cos t \\]\n\nWe need to calculate \\(\\frac{dx}{dt}\\) and \\(\\frac{dy}{dt}\\).\n\n---\n\n**Step 1:** Calculate \\(\\frac{dx}{dt}\\) and \\(\\frac{dy}{dt}**Solution:**\n\nFirst, find \\(\\frac{dx}{dt}\\):\n\n\\[ x = 2 \\sin t \\]\n\\[ \\frac{dx}{dt} = 2 \\cos t \\]\n\nNext, find \\(\\frac{dy}{dt}\\):\n\n\\[ y = 2 \\cos t \\]\n\\[ \\frac{dy}{dt} = -2 \\sin t \\]\n\n---\n\n**Step 2:** Substitute into the arc length formula:**\n\n\\[ L = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{\\left( 2 \\cos t \\right)^2 + \\left( -2 \\sin t \\right)^2} \\, dt \\]\n\nSimplify inside the square root:\n\n\\[ L = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{4 \\cos^2 t + 4 \\sin^2 t} \\, dt \\]\n\nSince \\(\\cos^2 t + \\sin^2 t = 1\\), the integral simplifies to:\n\n\\[ L = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{4 \\left( \\cos^2 t + \\sin^2 t \\right)} \\, dt \\]\n\nAgain simplify using \\(\\cos^2 t + \\sin^2 t = 1\\):\n\n\\[ L = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{4} \\, dt \\]\n\nSo:\n\n\\[ L = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} 2 \\, dt \\]\n\n---\n\n**Step 3:** Integrate from \\(0\\) to \\(\\pi**SOLUTION for Step 3:**\n\nEvaluate the integral:\n\n\\[ L = 2 \\int_{0}^{\\pi} dt \\]\n\nIntegrate \\(dt\\) directly:\n\n\\[ L = 2 \\left[ t \\right]_{0}^{\\pi} \\]\n\nEvaluating at the bounds:\n\n\\[ L = 2 (\\pi - 0) \\]\n\nThis leaves us with:\n\n\\[ L = 2 \\pi \\]\n\n---",
