      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the length of the parametric curve given by \\((x, y) = (2 \\sin t, 2 \\cos t)\\) from \\(t = 0\\) to \\(t = \\pi\\), we can use the arc length formula for parametric curves:\n\n\\[\nL = \\int_{a}^{b} \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{dx}{dt}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\frac{dy}{dt}\\right)^2} \\, dt\n\\]\n\nFirst, we need to find the derivatives of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) with respect to \\(t\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{dx}{dt} = \\frac{d}{dt}(2 \\sin t) = 2 \\cos t\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{dy}{dt} = \\frac{d}{dt}(2 \\cos t) = -2 \\sin t\n\\]\n\nNow we substitute these derivatives into the arc length formula:\n\n\\[\nL = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{(2 \\cos t)^2 + (-2 \\sin t)^2} \\, dt\n\\]\n\nSimplifying inside the square root:\n\n\\[\nL = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{4 \\cos^2 t + 4 \\sin^2 t} \\, dt = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{4 (\\cos^2 t + \\sin^2 t)} \\, dt\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\cos^2 t + \\sin^2 t = 1\\):\n\n\\[\nL = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{4 \\cdot 1} \\, dt = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} 2 \\, dt\n\\]\n\nNow we evaluate the integral:\n\n\\[\nL = 2 \\int_{0}^{\\pi} 1 \\, dt = 2 \\left[ t \\right]_{0}^{\\pi} = 2 (\\pi - 0) = 2\\pi\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the length of the parametric curve is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\pi}\n\\]\n\nLet's confirm this using Python.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\nfrom scipy.integrate import quad\n\n# Define the integrand function\ndef integrand(t):\n    return np.sqrt((2 * np.cos(t))**2 + (-2 * np.sin(t))**2)\n\n# Perform the integration from 0 to pi\nlength, error = quad(integrand, 0, np.pi)\nprint(length)\n```",
